WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:05.520
Welcome to the deep dive. You know, usually when you step into a completely new software environment,

00:06.400 --> 00:11.580
there is this underlying expectation that you are going to be starting entirely from scratch.

00:11.720 --> 00:15.840
Oh, absolutely. It can feel a bit like you've landed on an alien planet and you suddenly have

00:15.840 --> 00:19.680
to relearn how gravity works. Right. You expect to just toss out everything you know,

00:20.100 --> 00:24.460
pull up the documentation and, you know, learn an entirely new domain language from the ground up

00:24.460 --> 00:29.020
just to get a single service running. That is the standard onboarding experience for a lot of

00:29.020 --> 00:34.340
platforms. Yeah. But today, if you're a senior engineer who is already familiar with OSDU,

00:34.940 --> 00:40.280
the open subsurface data universe, and you are stepping into the Azure SPI ecosystem,

00:40.820 --> 00:45.220
it really isn't like that at all. No, it's not. The gravity is the exact same. The APIs,

00:45.380 --> 00:50.480
you know, are entirely familiar. Yeah. And data partitions still supply the core context for,

00:50.740 --> 00:54.900
well, basically everything you do. Because the fundamental shift happens entirely behind the

00:54.900 --> 00:59.580
scenes. And a lot of that shift is really driven by initiatives like the OSDU Venus effort.

00:59.720 --> 01:02.500
Right. The Venus effort. That's a huge piece of this puzzle.

01:02.500 --> 01:07.580
It really is. It moved towards separating cloud provider specific code out of the main GitLab

01:07.580 --> 01:14.360
repositories. So upstream OSDU now focuses on providing just a community reference implementation

01:14.360 --> 01:20.960
or CMPL. Which means if you are working on the Azure side, you know, specifically on the foundation

01:20.960 --> 01:26.540
that powers things like Microsoft's managed offering ADME or Azure Data Manager for Energy,

01:26.780 --> 01:29.860
you're managing a highly specialized Azure implementation.

01:30.240 --> 01:34.240
Exactly. But one that still has to plug perfectly into that upstream common core.

01:34.440 --> 01:38.720
Which brings us to our mission for this deep dive. We aren't just going to read down the list of

01:38.720 --> 01:41.780
cloud features or list off configuration file.

01:41.780 --> 01:46.300
No, that'd be incredibly boring. Very boring. Our goal is to help you build a complete,

01:46.660 --> 01:50.880
tangible mental model of how the code, the running environment, and the engineering system

01:50.880 --> 01:53.280
all interact. And to do that, we need a focal point.

01:53.620 --> 01:58.000
Right. We're going to anchor this entire conversation to one single central example,

01:58.420 --> 02:03.920
a simple partition lookup for opendes inside a development environment named dev1. We're going

02:03.920 --> 02:06.700
to follow that one single request on a complete round trip.

02:06.700 --> 02:12.680
I love this approach. Tracing a single request is, I mean, it's the absolute best way to take

02:12.680 --> 02:15.020
abstract cloud architecture and make it tangible.

02:15.220 --> 02:16.060
It grounds everything.

02:16.200 --> 02:20.840
Yeah. We'll trace that request into the running service, examine how the Azure provider handles it,

02:21.280 --> 02:27.240
trace a historical bug fix out to its repository, and then follow that code back through a build and

02:27.240 --> 02:28.900
into a live acceptance test.

02:28.900 --> 02:34.980
Okay. So let's unpack this. Before we can follow our opendes anywhere, we need to define

02:34.980 --> 02:36.440
the world it actually lives in.

02:36.700 --> 02:36.840
Yeah.

02:36.900 --> 02:40.260
And that starts with a term that gets thrown around constantly, SPI.

02:40.400 --> 02:41.220
Oh, SPI.

02:41.340 --> 02:47.580
Yes. As it turns out, SPI actually has three distinct, completely separate meanings in this

02:47.580 --> 02:48.040
ecosystem.

02:48.320 --> 02:53.540
It is so heavily overloaded, which causes a lot of initial confusion for new engineers. We really

02:53.540 --> 02:55.380
have to separate those definitions immediately.

02:55.520 --> 02:56.240
What's the first one?

02:56.240 --> 03:01.360
First, there is the service provider interface. This is an internal code boundary. It's the

03:01.360 --> 03:07.020
architectural contract where the common OSDU service code calls the Azure-specific implementation.

03:07.320 --> 03:11.580
And a critical detail here is that this is just a code contract, right? It's compiled into the

03:11.580 --> 03:12.040
same image.

03:12.340 --> 03:17.860
Exactly. It is not an extra server, and it is absolutely not an additional network hop.

03:18.120 --> 03:23.000
That distinction is huge for latency. It's just local code talking to local code inside the

03:23.000 --> 03:23.800
same running container.

03:23.800 --> 03:29.020
Right. Then we have the second meaning, which is the SPI stack. This is the deployment project.

03:29.460 --> 03:35.840
It corresponds to the osdu-spi-stack repository. And it represents the actual running development

03:35.840 --> 03:39.700
or test environment that you create when you use the SPI command line tool.

03:40.020 --> 03:42.180
Got it. So that's the stack. What's the third?

03:42.460 --> 03:47.740
The third meaning is the SPI engineering system. This corresponds to the osdu-spi repository.

03:48.080 --> 03:50.180
It does not run the application itself.

03:50.180 --> 03:51.400
So it's purely structural.

03:51.720 --> 03:57.240
Basically. It supplies all the reusable CICD templates, the GitHub Actions workflows, and,

03:57.400 --> 04:01.840
you know, the automation machinery used to build, integrate, and validate the code across all the

04:01.840 --> 04:02.640
other repositories.

04:02.880 --> 04:07.480
I always love mapping abstract architecture to real-world concepts. And this structure really

04:07.480 --> 04:09.520
reminds me of opening a franchise restaurant.

04:09.640 --> 04:11.520
Oh, I like that. How so?

04:11.520 --> 04:17.100
Well, the service provider interface. The first meaning, that's the corporate menu. It is the

04:17.100 --> 04:20.600
strict, uncompromising contract of what you have to serve to the customer.

04:20.720 --> 04:22.160
Right. You can't just invent a new burger.

04:22.280 --> 04:27.620
Exactly. Then the SPI stack. The second meaning is the physical restaurant building. It's the

04:27.620 --> 04:31.640
kitchen, the dining tables, and the specific street address where you operate.

04:31.740 --> 04:32.960
And the engineering system.

04:32.960 --> 04:36.500
The SPI engineering system is the corporate training manual. Yeah.

04:36.580 --> 04:41.860
And the supply chain workflows that every single franchise relies on to standardize how they

04:41.860 --> 04:43.260
actually build the burgers.

04:43.440 --> 04:48.640
The franchise analogy holds up incredibly well here, especially when you look at how three

04:48.640 --> 04:51.020
distinct repositories interact with each other.

04:51.180 --> 04:51.320
Yeah.

04:51.500 --> 04:56.280
Yeah. Because you have OSDU SPI stack acting as the construction crew creating that physical

04:56.280 --> 05:01.040
restaurant building. You have OSDU SPI providing the overarching corporate supply chain.

05:01.040 --> 05:03.540
And then the service fork, like osdu-spi-partition.

05:03.920 --> 05:08.020
Right. That service fork is the local franchise manager doing the actual work.

05:08.340 --> 05:15.020
It houses both the shared OSDU menu items and the Azure-specific kitchen equipment. And it's

05:15.020 --> 05:17.740
where you finally build the service image that gets deployed.

05:18.100 --> 05:24.000
Okay. So we have our interface, our stack, and our engineering system. Let's dig into what happens

05:24.000 --> 05:29.000
when you actually build that physical restaurant, the stack. Because the stack we were talking about

05:29.000 --> 05:31.660
is significantly larger than just a Kubernetes cluster, right?

05:31.660 --> 05:36.040
Oh, much larger. And the way the boundaries are drawn is a crucial part of the mental model.

05:36.040 --> 05:40.440
The environment we are calling dev1 lives inside an Azure resource group.

05:40.780 --> 05:40.820
Okay.

05:40.980 --> 05:45.280
Inside that group, you certainly have your AKS cluster, the Azure Kubernetes service.

05:45.380 --> 05:50.840
That cluster houses the OSDU namespace, the actual microservices, and platform middleware

05:50.840 --> 05:52.340
like Redis and PostgreSQL.

05:52.340 --> 05:57.040
But that cluster sits right alongside native external Azure data services?

05:57.200 --> 05:57.620
Exactly.

05:57.800 --> 06:02.400
Things like Cosmos DB, Azure Service Bus, and table storage. Those aren't stuffed inside

06:02.400 --> 06:03.260
Kubernetes containers.

06:03.460 --> 06:05.280
No, they are managed Azure services.

06:05.660 --> 06:10.120
Right. So the workloads running inside the AKS cluster have to reach out over the network

06:10.120 --> 06:13.820
to those external Azure resources via their native APIs.

06:14.180 --> 06:18.080
And understanding that separation really helps clarify the naming convention here.

06:18.080 --> 06:24.380
dev1 is the overarching environment, but opendes is just one specific data partition running

06:24.380 --> 06:24.940
inside it.

06:25.240 --> 06:31.380
Right. And because opendes is a distinct data partition, it has its own dedicated Cosmos DB

06:31.380 --> 06:37.100
SQL account, its own distinct Azure storage account, and its own service bus namespace, entirely

06:37.100 --> 06:38.700
isolated within this stack.

06:39.040 --> 06:44.280
Meanwhile, other resources like the table storage residing in the common storage account are shared

06:44.280 --> 06:46.040
globally by the entire environment.

06:46.040 --> 06:50.440
Okay. So let's put ourselves in the shoes of a developer. I'm sitting at my local workstation

06:50.440 --> 06:54.780
completely outside this cloud environment, and I run the creation command in my terminal.

06:55.420 --> 06:56.840
spi up --env dev1.

06:56.940 --> 06:58.420
All right. The classic up command.

06:58.760 --> 07:02.960
The CLI starts firing off Bicep templates to provision all those Azure resources, and it

07:02.960 --> 07:07.320
preps the Kubernetes manifests. Eventually, the CLI finishes running, and I get a successful

07:07.320 --> 07:08.680
green check mark on my terminal.

07:08.960 --> 07:09.640
A great feeling.

07:09.720 --> 07:15.920
It is. But if I immediately fire up Postman, or curl, to make an API call to the partition

07:15.920 --> 07:22.780
service, it fails. Why can't I immediately start making API calls if the CLI says my stack is up?

07:23.460 --> 07:28.000
That goes back to a really critical difference in modern cloud orchestration. The difference

07:28.000 --> 07:30.120
between declared state and observed state.

07:30.240 --> 07:31.460
Okay. Break that down for me.

07:31.560 --> 07:36.360
The green check mark you see from the CLI simply means the infrastructure as code orchestration

07:36.360 --> 07:42.080
was accepted. Bicep successfully created the Azure resources, and the desired Kubernetes

07:42.080 --> 07:44.680
configurations were committed or seeded.

07:45.240 --> 07:46.440
So it's just the plan.

07:46.620 --> 07:51.220
Exactly. That is merely the declared state. You have successfully updated the blueprint of

07:51.220 --> 07:52.000
what should be running.

07:52.220 --> 07:56.440
Ah. So the blueprint is updated, but the construction crew is still out there hammering nails.

07:56.700 --> 07:56.980
Mm-hmm.

07:57.100 --> 08:01.560
The observed state, what is actually running in the cluster, is lagging behind.

08:01.620 --> 08:07.300
The underlying mechanism there relies on a GitOps controller called Flux. Behind the scenes, Flux is

08:07.300 --> 08:12.020
continuously polling for those declared configurations and applying them to the Kubernetes cluster layer

08:12.020 --> 08:12.580
by layer.

08:12.680 --> 08:14.460
And Kubernetes itself is busy too, right?

08:14.840 --> 08:19.140
Right. Simultaneously, Kubernetes controllers are reacting to those configurations.

08:19.720 --> 08:25.200
They're polling container images, spinning up pods, handling transient network failures,

08:25.560 --> 08:27.540
and initializing partition data.

08:27.800 --> 08:29.160
That's a lot going on at once.

08:29.280 --> 08:34.060
All of these asynchronous overlapping responsibilities require time to resolve.

08:34.060 --> 08:39.260
That means the terminal returning control to my prompt and the API actually being usable

08:39.260 --> 08:43.060
to a client are two entirely disconnected events.

08:43.180 --> 08:47.740
Absolutely. Reconciliation is the term for that ongoing background work of bringing the

08:47.740 --> 08:50.180
observed state into alignment with the declared state.

08:50.260 --> 08:51.260
So you just have to wait.

08:51.560 --> 08:57.300
Because reconciliation takes time, a successful creation command from your CLI does not prove

08:57.300 --> 09:03.040
the partition service is actually running. Only a fully authenticated API response successfully

09:03.040 --> 09:06.140
traveling through the client boundary proves true readiness.

09:06.500 --> 09:10.300
Here's where it gets really interesting. Let's fast forward and assume the environment has

09:10.300 --> 09:11.740
fully reconciled and is ready.

09:11.840 --> 09:12.920
Okay. The API is up.

09:13.080 --> 09:14.540
Let's trace our API request.

09:15.400 --> 09:20.180
A client sends a GET request to /api/partition/v1/partitions/opendes.

09:20.640 --> 09:25.240
The request hits the API gateway. It routes successfully to the partition service pod.

09:25.620 --> 09:29.700
And the common OSDU code executes that internal interface we talked about earlier.

09:29.700 --> 09:36.140
IPartitionService.getPartition. Right. And at that exact moment, the common code stops and the Azure

09:36.140 --> 09:38.780
provider implementation takes over. And what does it do first?

09:39.120 --> 09:43.900
Well, to retrieve that partition configuration, the Azure provider doesn't immediately go to the

09:43.900 --> 09:47.820
database. It first checks its local cache. Makes sense for performance.

09:47.980 --> 09:53.200
Exactly. If there is a cache miss or the data has expired, it then reaches out over the network to

09:53.200 --> 09:57.780
the durable table storage located in the common Azure storage account, retrieves the configuration,

09:58.320 --> 09:59.740
and hands it back up the chain.

10:00.080 --> 10:04.700
You know, there is a brilliant historical fix in this specific flow that perfectly demonstrates how

10:04.700 --> 10:11.640
Azure SPI engineering handles failures. It's a commit from July 30, 2026, commit fc2dfbf.

10:11.640 --> 10:20.320
Oh, I know that commit. Prior to this fix, if the cache threw an exception, say a transient network blip

10:20.320 --> 10:26.760
between the service and the Redis cache, it would just crash the entire partition lookup request.

10:27.000 --> 10:32.220
Which is terrible. The real danger in that scenario is that a cache is strictly an optimization layer.

10:32.380 --> 10:34.020
Right. It's not the actual data store.

10:34.020 --> 10:40.200
Exactly. The durable table storage always has the authoritative answer. Bringing down the entire

10:40.200 --> 10:44.180
application because an optimization failed is a severe anti-pattern.

10:44.220 --> 10:45.000
So what do they do?

10:45.200 --> 10:50.880
So the engineers introduced guarded cache operations. If a cache read or write operation throws an

10:50.880 --> 10:56.160
exception today, the provider logs a warning telemetry event, treats the failure as a standard

10:56.160 --> 11:00.780
cache miss, and safely falls back to reading the data directly from the durable table storage.

11:00.880 --> 11:02.680
I do want to challenge this design slightly, though.

11:02.680 --> 11:03.080
Go for it.

11:03.320 --> 11:07.280
If the durable table storage works perfectly fine as a backup, and it has the authoritative

11:07.280 --> 11:12.100
data anyway, why bother logging the cache error at all? I mean, why not just quietly pretend

11:12.100 --> 11:16.620
the cache check succeeded, fetch the data from the table, and spare the operations team from

11:16.620 --> 11:18.300
a dashboard full of warning logs?

11:18.620 --> 11:25.240
Think about the alternative there. Quietly swallowing errors masks underlying systemic risk.

11:25.420 --> 11:26.780
Because you wouldn't know it's broken.

11:26.960 --> 11:32.000
Right. That violates a core observability principle in reliable engineering.

11:32.000 --> 11:36.600
You should absolutely recover from a failure when you have a valid alternative, but you

11:36.600 --> 11:39.360
must make that recovery observable to the engineers.

11:39.820 --> 11:42.300
So the warning log isn't noise. It's vital evidence.

11:42.480 --> 11:42.680
Yeah.

11:42.880 --> 11:47.480
If you just pretend everything is fine, your Redis cache could be completely broken for three

11:47.480 --> 11:47.840
months.

11:48.100 --> 11:49.200
And you'd never know.

11:49.320 --> 11:54.500
Exactly. Your system would be silently falling back to the slower database on every single

11:54.500 --> 11:59.880
request, degrading performance across the board, and you would have absolutely no idea why.

11:59.880 --> 12:04.620
You cannot fix a failing component if the system is actively hiding the failure from you.

12:04.800 --> 12:05.740
That makes total sense.

12:06.100 --> 12:10.120
This philosophy also explains why we rely on different scopes of evidence during testing.

12:10.820 --> 12:15.380
Unit tests are written to verify that this specific fallback logic correctly catches the

12:15.380 --> 12:17.100
exception in isolated code.

12:17.220 --> 12:18.720
But that's not enough on its own.

12:19.080 --> 12:23.540
No. Live acceptance tests are required to verify that the fully assembled service can actually

12:23.540 --> 12:29.620
reach out to the real table storage, authenticate, and answer the API in a live Azure environment.

12:29.820 --> 12:30.760
Which is a perfect segue.

12:31.100 --> 12:37.540
That cache fix is incredibly smart, but it lives in a highly specific Azure-only directory within

12:37.540 --> 12:41.600
the code base, specifically the provider/partition-azure path.

12:41.660 --> 12:43.720
Which means it is fork-owned source code.

12:43.820 --> 12:43.980
Right.

12:43.980 --> 12:49.800
And earlier we mentioned the OSDU Venus effort, where the upstream community separated out the

12:49.800 --> 12:50.880
cloud-specific code.

12:51.080 --> 12:57.540
So how does the engineering system protect that Azure-specific fix when the upstream OSDU community

12:57.540 --> 13:00.760
pushes a massive update to the shared common code base?

13:00.960 --> 13:01.940
It's a tricky balance.

13:02.120 --> 13:06.320
If upstream completely deletes or restructures their directory tree, how do we make sure our

13:06.320 --> 13:11.440
fork doesn't blindly copy that deletion and wipe out our hard-won cache fix during a Git merge?

13:11.440 --> 13:14.960
To understand that, we have to look at the dual mandate of the service fork.

13:15.480 --> 13:18.440
It has to juggle two conflicting responsibilities simultaneously.

13:19.140 --> 13:19.360
Okay.

13:19.460 --> 13:19.900
What are they?

13:20.160 --> 13:25.600
It must continuously receive shared upstream improvements while strictly maintaining its

13:25.600 --> 13:27.140
own Azure implementation.

13:27.660 --> 13:33.160
To resolve this conflict without endless merge nightmares, the engineering system uses a rigid

13:33.160 --> 13:34.580
three-branch strategy.

13:34.900 --> 13:37.080
Let's map out how those branches actually work.

13:37.360 --> 13:39.340
First, you have the fork upstream branch.

13:39.340 --> 13:43.560
This branch is generated directly from the upstream OSDU code.

13:43.760 --> 13:43.900
Right.

13:44.140 --> 13:48.380
But the critical mechanical step here is that the automation script generating this branch

13:48.380 --> 13:53.700
explicitly and structurally excludes the Azure provider code path entirely.

13:53.920 --> 13:56.780
The script strips it out completely before a commit is even made.

13:56.840 --> 13:58.660
Then you have the fork integration branch.

13:59.000 --> 14:04.020
This is the messy workspace where the generated upstream code and your current Azure code meet.

14:04.220 --> 14:08.360
And finally, you have the main branch, which is the protected, peer-reviewed final result.

14:08.360 --> 14:12.220
The structural omission in that first branch is kind of the genius of the system, isn't it?

14:12.420 --> 14:13.140
It really is.

14:13.200 --> 14:17.680
Because the Azure provider directory simply does not exist in the generated fork upstream branch,

14:18.140 --> 14:23.220
an upstream deletion or refactor cannot accidentally wipe out the Azure code maintained in the fork.

14:23.440 --> 14:25.160
Because Git doesn't even know it's missing.

14:25.300 --> 14:25.820
Exactly.

14:25.820 --> 14:37.000
When you merge fork upstream into fork integration, Git doesn't see a deletion conflict for the Azure folder because the upstream branch never claimed to own it in the first place.

14:37.720 --> 14:41.100
The system uses Git's own mechanics to protect your ownership.

14:41.380 --> 14:47.400
It's like a landlord deciding to update the plumbing in an entire apartment building.

14:47.460 --> 14:48.100
Okay, I'm following.

14:48.100 --> 14:51.740
The upstream code is the landlord's new plumbing system.

14:52.700 --> 15:02.520
The fork integration branch is the physical apartment where you have to make sure the landlord's new pipes don't accidentally get smashed through the custom-built furniture you installed.

15:02.600 --> 15:04.580
And that furniture is your Azure code.

15:04.700 --> 15:05.000
Yes.

15:05.620 --> 15:08.980
The structural omission guarantees your furniture stays exactly where you put it.

15:09.040 --> 15:10.960
That analogy paints the exact picture.

15:10.960 --> 15:18.760
But, you know, to take that a step further, protecting your custom furniture from getting smashed doesn't mean the landlord's new pipes will automatically connect to your sink.

15:18.840 --> 15:19.480
Oh, that's true.

15:19.860 --> 15:23.340
The structural omission protects the physical files of your Azure provider.

15:23.660 --> 15:27.620
But the engineers are still entirely responsible for the actual integration.

15:28.480 --> 15:33.320
If upstream changed a core interface, you still have to rewrite your Azure code to match it.

15:33.460 --> 15:37.340
So the system protects your property, but it doesn't do the plumbing for you.

15:37.400 --> 15:39.440
You still have to break out the wrench and plumb the sink.

15:39.560 --> 15:40.040
Good to know.

15:40.040 --> 15:43.740
Now, let's say our code survives that integration process.

15:43.940 --> 15:46.180
It gets built into a container image.

15:46.860 --> 15:48.720
I love this concept in cloud engineering.

15:49.480 --> 15:51.720
A built image is just a candidate.

15:52.140 --> 15:53.440
It is not a conclusion.

15:53.720 --> 15:55.240
It's just potential at that point.

15:55.380 --> 15:55.580
Right.

15:55.840 --> 16:05.720
How do we safely test this candidate image in a live environment without spinning up a massive, expensive, time-consuming new Azure stack for every single pull request?

16:05.980 --> 16:08.780
We use a deployment pattern known as borrow, prove, restore.

16:08.780 --> 16:15.400
This is the mechanism that allows a standing development stack to support live acceptance testing for individual services.

16:15.560 --> 16:16.440
Okay. Walk me through that.

16:16.440 --> 16:22.160
Let's say your dev1 environment normally runs a stable version of the partition service, which we will call Image A.

16:22.580 --> 16:24.440
You have just built a new candidate, Image B.

16:24.440 --> 16:31.600
And when we talk about images in the system, we are strictly talking about the cryptographic digest, not just a readable tag like Latest or V2.

16:31.720 --> 16:32.460
Oh, absolutely.

16:32.940 --> 16:37.640
Relying on readable tags in CICD is incredibly dangerous because tags can be overwritten.

16:38.200 --> 16:42.580
The digest guarantees we are testing the exact immutable binary candidate.

16:42.580 --> 16:44.940
So what happens with Image B's digest?

16:45.240 --> 16:52.720
The workflow takes the digest for Image B and writes it into a specific Kubernetes config map inside the cluster called osdu-image-lock.

16:53.000 --> 16:55.780
That write operation is the deployment input.

16:56.360 --> 17:02.180
And Flux, our trusty GitOps reconciler from earlier, notices that the Image Lock config map has changed.

17:02.360 --> 17:02.600
Yes.

17:02.860 --> 17:03.940
It spots the update.

17:03.940 --> 17:07.520
It selects Image B, pulls it down, and rolls it out to the partition pods.

17:07.840 --> 17:19.080
So we have effectively borrowed the live environment just for our specific service, while the rest of the stack continues to supply the real Cosmos DB, the real table storage, and all the other surrounding microservices.

17:19.480 --> 17:23.340
The testing workflow then verifies that Image B is actually running and healthy.

17:24.120 --> 17:30.840
Once confirmed, it executes the suite of API acceptance tests declared in the service's .spi/service.yaml file.

17:30.980 --> 17:31.700
The proof phase.

17:32.020 --> 17:32.540
Exactly.

17:32.540 --> 17:38.080
If the API answers correctly, you have proven the candidate works in reality, not just in theory.

17:38.360 --> 17:39.340
That is the proof phase.

17:39.780 --> 17:43.200
Finally, we execute the restore phase to give the environment back.

17:43.460 --> 17:46.260
This is where I have another major question about shared systems.

17:46.420 --> 17:47.040
Lay it on me.

17:47.160 --> 17:52.560
What if my test fails dramatically and the GitHub Actions runner crashes before it can restore?

17:53.480 --> 18:02.340
Or what if I start my test and 10 seconds later, another engineer jumps in and overwrites my pin in the image lock with their own candidate?

18:02.540 --> 18:05.700
Image C. Does the environment just stay permanently broken?

18:06.080 --> 18:09.220
That brings up a crucial vulnerability in shared state management.

18:09.860 --> 18:13.380
Restoration in this system is not a blind, unconditional promise.

18:13.380 --> 18:14.540
How does it protect itself?

18:14.540 --> 18:20.940
The workflow will only write the original stable image A back to the lock if it can prove it still owns the temporary pin.

18:21.160 --> 18:21.380
Ah.

18:21.860 --> 18:24.940
So when my workflow wakes up to restore, it checks the metadata.

18:25.140 --> 18:25.320
Right.

18:25.480 --> 18:33.740
If it sees that another engineer's run came in and overwrote my pin to test image C, my older workflow realizes it's no longer the owner and it just backs away.

18:33.740 --> 18:35.400
It leaves the config map alone.

18:35.760 --> 18:43.080
If it were to blindly restore image A without checking ownership, it would actively destroy the newer test that is currently running for your colleague.

18:43.480 --> 18:44.920
Which would be super frustrating.

18:45.180 --> 18:45.460
Very.

18:45.960 --> 18:48.720
The system defaults to respecting the current owner.

18:49.340 --> 18:54.920
Now, if your runner completely crashes and is lost, a pin might be left behind indefinitely.

18:55.160 --> 18:55.640
And then one.

18:55.640 --> 19:00.980
In that specific edge case, restoration becomes an operation that requires human inspection.

19:01.580 --> 19:06.320
A developer has to manually step in, figure out whose test died, and reset the lock.

19:06.640 --> 19:06.900
Okay.

19:07.020 --> 19:10.040
So what does this all mean for access and security?

19:10.540 --> 19:20.940
Because borrowing a live environment, altering Kubernetes config maps, and making all these external Azure data requests requires incredibly strict access control.

19:20.980 --> 19:21.800
It really does.

19:21.800 --> 19:26.660
Navigating this ecosystem requires understanding three completely separate identity types.

19:27.420 --> 19:31.420
Success in one type of identity relationship does not guarantee success in the others.

19:31.580 --> 19:36.920
When an operation fails, isolating which of the three relationships broke is usually step one.

19:36.980 --> 19:37.140
Okay.

19:37.180 --> 19:37.760
What's the first one?

19:38.040 --> 19:40.700
First, you have the client calling OSDU.

19:41.220 --> 19:45.740
This is an incoming API request from a user or an external application.

19:45.740 --> 19:54.740
The system handles authentication verifying who you are via a JWT token and authorization verifying what you are allowed to do.

19:55.120 --> 19:57.020
And that authorization is handled locally.

19:57.260 --> 19:59.660
Interestingly, yes, authorization is service specific.

19:59.980 --> 20:03.760
The partition service, for example, utilizes an app-only caller check.

20:04.140 --> 20:10.880
It does not perform a massive entitlements group lookup on every single request because that would just kill performance.

20:10.880 --> 20:11.220
Okay.

20:11.340 --> 20:12.360
Second identity type.

20:13.080 --> 20:14.180
The service calling Azure.

20:14.280 --> 20:18.180
This is our partition service reaching out to that durable table storage.

20:18.360 --> 20:18.640
Right.

20:18.780 --> 20:21.460
It uses workload identity to project a short-lived token.

20:21.980 --> 20:28.820
That means we completely avoid having to store static Azure data service keys or connection strings in configuration files.

20:28.940 --> 20:32.580
Moving away from static connection strings eliminates a massive attack factor.

20:32.700 --> 20:33.260
Huge win.

20:33.260 --> 20:36.180
And the third type is the workflow changing the test environment.

20:36.700 --> 20:44.920
When our borrow, prove, restore workflow reaches out from GitHub to update that OSDU image lock config map, it uses a federated deployment identity.

20:44.980 --> 20:45.640
And that's restricted.

20:46.020 --> 20:46.320
Yes.

20:46.420 --> 20:52.740
The crucial security mechanism here is that its permissions are narrowly scoped only to that specific image lock object.

20:53.060 --> 20:56.480
It cannot alter databases or change other services.

20:56.880 --> 20:59.880
Let me pause you there because I want to make sure I'm visualizing this correctly.

21:00.060 --> 21:00.280
Sure.

21:00.280 --> 21:16.740
If we are using workload identity to access Azure resources without static keys and our deployment workflows use scoped, federated identities to talk to Kubernetes, does that mean this entire Azure SPI stack is completely passwordless?

21:16.880 --> 21:20.600
That is a highly pervasive misconception, but it is actually incorrect.

21:20.760 --> 21:21.100
Really?

21:21.320 --> 21:21.520
Yeah.

21:21.700 --> 21:23.860
Passwords absolutely still exist within the cluster.

21:23.860 --> 21:29.240
For example, middleware components like Redis or PostgreSQL still rely on internal credentials.

21:29.240 --> 21:31.960
Furthermore, it is vital to remember the context.

21:32.160 --> 21:34.260
This is a shared development and test environment.

21:34.420 --> 21:34.660
Meaning.

21:34.800 --> 21:39.620
The various OSDU workloads running inside the cluster often share a broad Azure identity.

21:39.880 --> 21:45.700
Meaning that one service's access to Azure is not strictly network isolated from a sibling service running right next to it.

21:46.020 --> 21:46.560
Precisely.

21:47.140 --> 21:58.280
A narrowly scoped deployment permission limits who can update the image lock, but it does not mean an untrusted malicious service image is magically rendered harmless once it is running inside the cluster.

21:58.280 --> 22:00.100
Because they're effectively roommates.

22:00.300 --> 22:00.460
Right.

22:00.900 --> 22:05.720
This environment architecture relies on the fact that it is shared by trusted engineering participants.

22:06.160 --> 22:11.780
It is not fundamentally designed to isolate mutually untrusted code from hostile actors.

22:12.000 --> 22:13.920
Okay, let's bring this whole journey full circle.

22:14.640 --> 22:16.080
We started with a very simple premise.

22:16.760 --> 22:20.020
A client looking up the opendes in the dev1 environment.

22:20.240 --> 22:21.900
A single basic request.

22:21.900 --> 22:29.020
We followed that request down into the running stack, moving past the CLI's declared state, and into the reality of the observed state.

22:29.140 --> 22:40.480
We examined how the Azure provider intercepts that request, and we explored a real-world cache fallback fix that safely relies on durable table storage while fiercely maintaining the observability of failures.

22:40.480 --> 22:49.280
We traced how that Azure-specific source code is structurally protected from massive upstream community changes through careful Git branch integration.

22:49.440 --> 22:50.580
The landlord and the plumbing.

22:50.960 --> 22:51.280
Exactly.

22:51.620 --> 23:03.260
We learned how it gets built into a candidate image digest, and how the CICD workflow borrows the live environment, proves the code works, and attempts a highly coordinated ownership-aware restoration.

23:03.260 --> 23:08.420
And finally, we mapped out the three distinct identities making all of this possible.

23:08.940 --> 23:15.020
The client authenticating to the API, the workload federating to Azure, and the deployment workflow mutating the cluster.

23:15.260 --> 23:18.940
This is the complete mental model you need for Azure SPI engineering.

23:19.600 --> 23:22.240
You do not need to memorize every line of Bicep code.

23:22.660 --> 23:26.460
You just need to understand where the boundaries are drawn and how state is managed across them.

23:26.680 --> 23:29.600
It really changes how you look at the entire system.

23:29.600 --> 23:30.000
It does.

23:30.000 --> 23:33.660
But it leaves me with one final, slightly provocative thought to chew on.

23:33.760 --> 23:34.060
Oh.

23:34.500 --> 23:39.040
This entire engineering ecosystem relies heavily on that borrow-prove-restore loop.

23:39.400 --> 23:45.680
It depends entirely on workflows, politely checking metadata, and respecting who owns the PIN in a shared environment.

23:45.840 --> 23:48.300
It requires a tremendous amount of asynchronous coordination.

23:48.820 --> 23:49.220
Exactly.

23:49.680 --> 23:56.380
As these development and test environments become increasingly automated, and as continuous integration pipelines speed up even more,

23:56.380 --> 24:05.720
what happens when dozens or maybe hundreds of transient workflows have to negotiate state, ownership, and clean up entirely without human operators?

24:05.980 --> 24:07.660
That's a scary thought.

24:07.900 --> 24:08.200
Right.

24:08.600 --> 24:12.980
If a lost runner leaves a PIN behind today, a human developer investigates it.

24:13.480 --> 24:19.620
But tomorrow, will the concept of declared state eventually become so chaotic and fast-paced

24:19.620 --> 24:26.000
that we will need an AI arbiter just to manage who actually owns a test environment at any given millisecond?

24:26.120 --> 24:32.620
When the scale of automation outpaces human oversight, the systems that govern that automation have to evolve just as aggressively.

24:33.000 --> 24:34.800
It's a fascinating problem to anticipate.

24:35.040 --> 24:38.260
Something for all you senior engineers to mull over the next time you push a commit.

24:38.720 --> 24:41.940
Watch that green checkmark appear and wait for the real world to catch up.

24:42.500 --> 24:45.200
Thanks for joining us on this deep dive into Azure SPI.

24:45.300 --> 24:45.900
We'll catch you next time.

